        "scanners": {
          "vt": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "analysis": "Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: kubernetes\nVersion: 1.0.4\n\nThe skill bundle provides a highly sophisticated and well-documented multi-agent framework for Kubernetes and OpenShift operations. However, it is classified as suspicious due to several high-risk patterns and critical vulnerabilities. Multiple scripts (e.g., log-search.sh, metric-query.sh, and slo-report.sh) use 'eval' to execute curl commands constructed from environment variables and agent-provided arguments, creating a significant shell injection surface. Additionally, skill-improvement-pr.sh implements an automated 'self-improvement' logic that parses logs to programmatically create git branches and pull requests; in an agentic environment, this could be exploited to automate the introduction of malicious code into the repository if the agent's logging process is compromised. The bundle also requests an extremely broad set of high-privilege credentials (KUBECONFIG, Cloud provider keys, Vault, and GitHub tokens) which, combined with the execution vulnerabilities, presents a high risk.",
            "source": "palm",
            "checkedAt": 1774192506338
          },
          "skillspector": null,
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "high",
            "summary": "The package appears to implement a plausible Kubernetes multi-agent toolset, but registry metadata, install claims, and runtime instructions are inconsistent and the skill asks for broad cloud and cluster credentials and to execute third‑party scripts — review carefully before installing.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The declared capabilities (multi-agent Kubernetes/OpenShift ops: kubectl/oc, ArgoCD, image scanning, backups, RBAC, etc.) align with the files and scripts included. However the registry metadata (no required env vars or binaries) contradicts the SKILL.md which lists many required env vars and binaries; that mismatch is unexpected and should be treated as suspicious metadata hygiene.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The SKILL.md instructs cloning and running scripts from a third‑party GitHub repository and the included scripts perform cluster and cloud operations (namespace provisioning, resource creation, backups, image scans, promotions). The instructions reference reading KUBECONFIG and cloud provider credentials, writing audit/log files in the repo (LOGS.md, MEMORY.md), and running potentially destructive actions (the docs explicitly warn some scripts perform deletions). Although guardrails and human approval rules are documented, the runtime instructions give the agent latitude to access many sensitive resources and to write files — verify these scripts before executing.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Registry metadata claims 'No install spec' / instruction-only, but SKILL.md and README recommend 'npx skills add' which will pull and install code from https://github.com/kcns008/cluster-agent-swarm-skills. That means code will be written to disk and executed. The source is a GitHub repo (a known host) but installs are not pinned by default; the SKILL.md itself recommends pinning to a tag/commit. Unpinned scripted installs from third‑party repos increase risk — review and pin before installing.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill requests a broad set of sensitive credentials and env vars (KUBECONFIG, AWS_* keys, AZURE_* service principal, GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS, ARGOCD_AUTH_TOKEN, VAULT_TOKEN, GITHUB_TOKEN, QUAY_PASSWORD, DOCKER_CONFIG, etc.). Those appear relevant to the multi-cloud, multi-cluster capabilities, but requiring all of them upfront (rather than optional per-provider) is disproportionate and increases blast radius. Also the registry metadata advertised 'none' for required env vars while SKILL.md lists many — a clear inconsistency that must be clarified.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The swarm is designed to persist state in repository files (LOGS.md, MEMORY.md, INCIDENTS.md) and the SKILL.md explicitly documents agents committing changes to those files. always=false and model invocation is permitted (normal). Persistent state and repo write access are expected for this kind of coordination agent, but they increase the impact if credentials or scripts are malicious — limit repository write permissions and ensure human‑approval gates are enforced.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "note"
              }
            ],
            "guidance": "This skill package contains many scripts and explicit instructions to clone and run code from a third‑party GitHub repo and to use broad cloud and cluster credentials. Before installing: 1) Do not run unpinned installs — clone the repo and pin to a specific commit or release; 2) Manually review the scripts (especially anything that calls kubectl/oc/argocd/aws/az/gcloud and any 'delete' or 'promote' actions) and verify the human-approval enforcement; 3) Limit credentials: provide least-privilege service accounts and per-provider creds only when needed (don't hand over all cloud keys to test installs); 4) Test in an isolated/dev cluster first (never start in production); 5) Restrict repository write access and ensure audit/logging is captured externally; 6) If you cannot review the scripts yourself, request a security/code review or refuse installation. The primary red flags are the metadata vs SKILL.md inconsistencies and the unpinned scripted install combined with broad credential requests.",
